                <div class="c-article-body">
                    <section aria-labelledby="Abs1" data-title="Abstract" lang="en"><div class="c-article-section" id="Abs1-section"><h2 class="c-article-section__title js-section-title js-c-reading-companion-sections-item" id="Abs1">Abstract</h2><div class="c-article-section__content" id="Abs1-content"><p>Inland waters are major sources of greenhouse gases (GHGs) to the atmosphere. In this Review, we compile and reconcile current global estimates of CO<sub>2</sub>, CH<sub>4</sub> and N<sub>2</sub>O emissions from inland waters and place them in the context of the global GHG budget. Estimates indicate total inland water emissions of approximately 8.2 (6.5–10.6; 90% CI) Pg CO<sub>2</sub>-eq. yr<sup>−1</sup> over a 100-year time horizon, despite inland waters only covering less than 3% of the land surface. Mapping of global emission patterns reveals that the largest emitters of GHGs are low-latitude river networks and the numerous small lakes of the Northern Hemisphere. High latitudes, the tropics and very small water bodies have a major role in global fluxes but remain under-represented in observational datasets. Drawing on modelling and budget analyses, we estimate that emissions have increased by approximately 2.8 (2.3–3.5; 90% CI) Pg CO<sub>2</sub>-eq. yr<sup>−</sup><sup>1</sup> since the Industrial Revolution, equivalent to an offset of approximately one-third of the land carbon sink. Global inland water GHG emissions are expected to keep increasing throughout the twenty-first century, mainly driven by climate change, but pollution control and careful dam planning could help to limit this increase.</p></div></div></section>
                    
    


                    
                        

                        
